{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:1a1acc35f91e9972f8fd2fb42e5a775cbf94be6a801eb0f2cacfcff1663b57f0","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.75},{"probability":0.9,"value":3}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PPP WEIGHT: w_China = 0.375, w_OECD = 0.625. Fermi 0.38 (reconciled against the adapter's independent 0.371 at 2031), Historian 0.39, Bayesian 0.375, Red-Team 0.37, Scout 0.31 — Scout's is the outlier and is inconsistent with OECD-38 PPP GDP ~$72T against China ~$41T; I down-weight him to zero on the weighting and note the Red-Teamer's correct rejoinder that identical weights over non-identical country sets is where a composition error HIDES rather than where it is ruled out. CHINA LEG = 2.75%/yr, and this is where the Red-Teamer landed his biggest real hit. The locked -2.7 pp/yr is an IMF 2025-2050 counterfactual decomposition against a no-aging world, not an adjustment to an aging-inclusive forecast, and Fermi's cross-check (\"a frictionless China counterfactual of ~4.7%/yr less the locked -2.7 pp = 2.0%\") subtracts it from a rate already produced by an economy whose working-age population has shrunk since 2012, whose property sector has already deleveraged, and whose pension subsidy is already 10.1% of fiscal expenditure. That cross-check is circular, so the \"two independent routes agreeing\" that carried the Bayesian's LR-2.0 update was partly illusory — the agreement was one route plus its own echo. I therefore CANCEL the cross-check while KEEPING Fermi's genuinely independent bottom-up route (labour -0.63, capital deepening +2.00, TFP +1.10, property/credit/pension-claim drag -0.50 = 1.97) and reading it as a lower bound, because hi